    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>This is probably the most widespread piece of fragrance advice in existence, which is unfortunate because it actively works against you. Rubbing your wrists together creates friction, which generates heat, which causes the top notes to evaporate faster than they would if you had just left them alone.</p><p>You are not locking anything in. You are speeding up the first phase of the fragrance and shortening the opening you just paid for. The wrist rub does not make your perfume last longer. It costs you the first ten minutes of wear.</p><p>Spray it. Let it dry. Walk out the door.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "editorial--text-section",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Myth 2: a higher price tag means it lasts longer",
            "heading_size": 56,
            "heading_size_mobile": 32,
            "heading_line_height": 1,
            "heading_color": "#121212",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 50,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 30,
            "column_1_text": "<p>This one actually costs real money. Longevity in a fragrance comes from concentration and formulation, not from the price tag. A well-made EDP at twenty dollars will last significantly longer than a designer EDT at two hundred, because the oil concentration is doing all the work, not the branding.</p><p>When you pay $300 for a designer fragrance, you are paying for the bottle, the packaging, the global campaign, the Sephora real estate, and the margins of everyone involved between the factory and your bathroom shelf. Some of that investment reaches the actual liquid. A lot of it does not.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p><a href=\"/products/addiction\" title=\"Addiction\"><strong>Addiction</strong> </a>(inspired by Baccarat Rouge 540) at EDP concentration lasts a full day on moisturized skin. Baccarat Rouge 540 at EDT concentration does not. Concentration is the variable that matters. Price is mostly noise.</p>",
            "body_size": 22,
            "body_size_mobile": 14,
            "body_line_height": 1.6,
            "body_color": "#000000",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 60,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1000,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#f9f6f1",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
