    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "editorial--content",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1200,
            "section_padding": 20,
            "background_color": "#f9f6f1",
            "heading": "<p>Myth 3: fragrance only works on pulse points</p>",
            "heading_size": 60,
            "heading_color": "#000000",
            "heading_spacing": 10,
            "box_color": "#8b1d22",
            "box_radius": 25,
            "box_padding": 40,
            "box_spacing": 10,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>Pulse points are genuinely worth using. Wrists, neck, inside of the elbow: they emit heat throughout the day, which activates the fragrance and keeps it developing. That is all real and worth doing.</p><p>But pulse points are not the only option. And treating them like a rule means you are missing some of the best application spots available. Hair holds fragrance for hours longer than most skin, because it has no oils to absorb and break down the scent molecules. A light mist through your hair before you leave the house is one of the most underrated longevity tricks in fragrance. Give your locks a spritz - you’ll be pleasantly surprised.</p><p>The back of the neck works great too, particularly for fragrances you want to leave a trail with. Clothing, applied carefully on fabrics that will not stain, gives you a completely different quality of wear. Pulse points are a great starting point. The rest is worth experimenting with.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#ffffff",
            "box_text_size": 22,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.6,
            "paragraph_spacing": 25,
            "subheading": "<p>Myth 4: if you cannot smell it, it is gone</p>",
            "subheading_size": 60,
            "subheading_color": "#000000",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>This is the myth that causes the most unnecessary reapplication, and the most accidental over-spraying. You put your fragrance on in the morning. By mid-morning it’s ghosted you. You assume it has faded and reach for the bottle again.</p><p>What has actually happened is olfactory adaptation. You’ve become nose blind. Your brain filters out constant background stimuli so you can focus on new sensory information. Your own scent becomes background noise. You stop registering it. Other people around you almost certainly still can. </p><p>Before you reapply, ask someone nearby if they can smell your fragrance. The answer is usually yes. Spraying again on top of something that is still there is how you end up being the person who smells like the inside of the limo transporting giddy seniors to prom.  </p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 22,
            "bottom_text_color": "#000000",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.6
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
    "blocks_jgTJWD": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_19f144a_kVmwE7": {
          "type": "editorial--staggered-typography",
          "settings": {
            "line_1_text": "Timeless Grace",
            "line_1_alignment": "left",
            "line_1_offset": 300,
            "line_2_text": "vs",
            "line_2_alignment": "center",
            "line_2_offset": 0,
            "line_3_text": "Coco Mademoiselle by Chanel:",
            "line_3_alignment": "right",
            "line_3_offset": -40,
            "body_text": "<p>Coco Mademoiselle is the benchmark for a certain kind of feminine fragrance. Clean, grown-up, and immediately recognisable. It has been the top-selling women's perfume in the UK and US for years. The EDP retails at around $160 for 100ml and it earns that position consistently.</p><p><strong>Timeless Grace</strong> opens with the same fresh orange and bergamot, develops through jasmine and rose, and settles into a warm patchouli base that wears throughout the day. The comparison test here produced the closest result of the three: on dry-down, both fragrances sat in the same warm, clean territory. The original has a very slight additional depth in the base that reflects the higher concentration of raw materials, but it is a difference you would only notice if you were specifically looking for it.<br/><br/>At around $20 versus $160, Timeless Grace makes a compelling case for anyone who loves the Coco Mademoiselle profile but wants to wear it every day without the creeping sense that they should be saving it for something more significant.</p>",
            "line_1_size": 44,
            "line_2_size": 85,
            "line_3_size": 44,
            "body_size": 22,
            "line_2_margin_top": 30,
            "line_3_margin_top": 4,
            "header_spacing": 40,
            "body_max_width": 1000,
            "section_padding_top": 60,
            "section_padding_bottom": 60,
            "background_color": "#ffffff",
            "text_color": "#121212"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_19f144a_kVmwE7"
      ],
      "disabled": true,
      "name": "Staggered typography",
      "settings": {}
