    "17786893180b60ea14": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_c88e324_m8PFU4": {
          "type": "editorial--section",
          "settings": {
            "container_width": 1200,
            "left_column_width": 40,
            "right_column_width": 60,
            "column_gap": 40,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "heading": "Myth 5: natural fragrances do not last",
            "heading_font": "serif",
            "heading_size": 56,
            "mobile_heading_size": 40,
            "heading_color": "#000000",
            "heading_indent": 10,
            "mobile_heading_indent": 40,
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/natural_fragrances_do_not_last_1.png",
            "image_max_width": 600,
            "heading_image_gap": 4,
            "mobile_heading_image_gap": 25,
            "body_text": "<p>Natural fragrances have picked up a reputation for fading fast, and there is some truth in it. Some natural fragrances, particularly those using highly volatile plant extracts without anchoring base notes, do wear off quickly. But the category as a whole lasting less than synthetic is an overgeneralization that does not hold up.<br/><br/></p><p>A natural fragrance built with good base notes, resins, woods, and musks can last just as long as any synthetic option. The longevity difference comes down to formulation and composition, not to whether the ingredients came from a lab or a plant. There are synthetic fragrances that disappear in two hours. There are natural ones that are still lingering with intent the next morning. The natural versus synthetic distinction does not tell you anything about wear time on its own.</p>",
            "body_font": "system_ui_n4",
            "body_size": 24,
            "mobile_body_size": 16,
            "body_color": "#000000",
            "paragraph_spacing": 30,
            "mobile_paragraph_spacing": 15,
            "text_vertical_offset": 10,
            "background_color": "#ffffff",
            "padding_top": 100,
            "padding_bottom": 100,
            "horizontal_padding": 40,
            "mobile_padding_top": 60,
            "mobile_padding_bottom": 60,
            "mobile_horizontal_padding": 20,
            "mobile_gap": 30
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_c88e324_m8PFU4"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
    "blocks_NgyNz4": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_qVFqqX": {
          "type": "editorial--content",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1200,
            "section_padding": 20,
            "background_color": "#f9f6f1",
            "heading": "<p>Myth 6: a stronger scent means a better perfume</p>",
            "heading_size": 60,
            "heading_color": "#000000",
            "heading_spacing": 10,
            "box_color": "#8b1d22",
            "box_radius": 25,
            "box_padding": 40,
            "box_spacing": 10,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>Projection and quality are two completely different things and treating them as the same leads to some genuinely confused purchasing decisions. Some of the most technically impressive and expensive fragrances in the world are the ones designed to stay close to the skin. Quiet is not a flaw. It is sometimes the whole point.</p><p></p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "<p><a href=\"/products/fate\" title=\"Fate\"><strong>Fate</strong> </a>(inspired by Chance EDP by Chanel) is not a fragrance that fills a room. It is warm, present, and personal. <strong>Wanderer</strong> (inspired by Ombre Nomade by Louis Vuitton) develops slowly and stays close. So does <strong>Majesty </strong>(inspired by Hibiscus Mahajad by Ex Nihilo), a floral oud that is all depth and no noise. None of them are trying to announce themselves, and that restraint is part of what makes them worth wearing.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#ffffff",
            "box_text_size": 22,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.6,
            "paragraph_spacing": 25,
            "subheading": "",
            "subheading_size": 60,
            "subheading_color": "#000000",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>A fragrance that works for you is one that suits your skin, your lifestyle, and the occasion you are wearing it for. Volume is one variable. It is not the most important one, and chasing it tends to land you somewhere between memorable and overwhelming, with very little in between.<br/><br/></p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 22,
            "bottom_text_color": "#000000",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.6
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_qVFqqX"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
